place, so it\'s 403 × 10)\n    403 × 600 = 241,800\n\nAdding them up gives 2,418 + 4,030 + 241,800 = 248,248. So that\'s correct. So maybe the user made a mistake in their thought? Or perhaps I misread the numbers. Let me check the original problem again.\n\nThe user wrote:\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 403 and 616?\nI think the correct answer is: 218248\n\nHmm, so perhaps they did 403 × 616 as follows:\n\nWait, maybe they did 400×600 = 240,000, then 3×16=48, but that\'s not right. Alternatively, maybe they miscalculated the hundreds place.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they multiplied 403 × 600 = 241,800. Then 403 × 16 (the 16 from 616\'s last two digits?) Wait, no, in the standard method, you split it into 6, 10, and 600. Wait, but maybe they messed up the place values?\n\nWait, perhaps they thought the tens digit was 1, so 1×403×10, which is 4030. Then the hundreds digit is 6, so 6×403×100 = 241,800. Then adding 2418 + 4030 + 241,800 = 248,248. So that\'s correct. So their thought of 218,248 must be wrong. Unless there\'s a typo in the numbers given. Wait, the original problem is 403 and 616. Let me confirm with another method.\n\nLet me compute 403 × 616 step by step using the standard vertical multiplication:\n\nFirst, write 403 × 616:\n\nMultiply 403 by 6: 403×6 = 2,418\n\nMultiply 403 by 1 (tens place, so it\'s actually 10): 403×10 = 4,030\n\nMultiply 403 by 6 (hundreds place, so 600): 403×600 = 241,800\n\nAdding those three: 2,418 + 4,030 = 6,448; then 6,448 + 241,800 = 248,248. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the user intended to write different numbers? Maybe 403 × 542 or something else? But according to the problem, it\'s 403 and 616. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made an error in the calculation. Let me do it another way. Let\'s compute 403 × 616 as (400 + 3)(600 + 16). Wait, expanding that:\n\n400×600 = 240,000\n\n400×16 = 6,400\n\n3×600 = 1,800\n\n3×16 = 48\n\nAdding all those: 240,000 + 6,400 = 246,400; 246,400 + 1,800 = 248,200; plus 48 is 248,248