Wait, maybe the user made a mistake? Let me verify using another method.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can compute 955 \u00d7 366 using another approach. Let's see.\n\n955 \u00d7366. Let me break down 366 into 300 +60 +6, which is what I did before, so the same as partial products.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the distributive property:\n\n(900 +55) \u00d7 (300 +60 +6).\n\nBut that might complicate, but let me try.\n\nAlternatively, compute 955 \u00d7 366 as:\n\nFirst, compute 955 \u00d7 300 =286,500.\n\nThen 955 \u00d760 =57,300.\n\nThen 955 \u00d76 =5,730.\n\nAdding them gives the same as before: 286,500 +57,300 =343,800; then plus 5,730 gives 343,800+5,730=349,530. Same result. So the correct answer is 349,530. The user thought it was 349,500, which is 30 less. So likely a miscalculation in their thought process.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user made an error in adding the partial products. Let me see:\n\nSuppose someone added 5730 +57,300 +286,500.\n\nWait, 5730 +57,300 =63,030, then adding 286,500: 63,030 +286,500. If they thought 63,000 +286,500 =349,500 and then subtract the 30? Not sure. Alternatively, maybe they miscalculated the hundreds place?\n\nWait, let me check the hundreds place again. The hundreds digit is 3, so 3\u00d7100. So 955 \u00d7300. 955\u00d73 is 2865, so \u00d7100 is 286,500. Correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user thought the hundreds place was 300, so 3\u00d7955 is 2865, but then added incorrectly?\n\nAlternatively, maybe they multiplied 955 \u00d7 366 as (950+5)(360+6) or some other way?\n\nWait, let me compute using another method for verification.\n\nLet me do 955 \u00d7 366.\n\nAlternatively, use the standard vertical multiplication:\n\n       955\n\n     \u00d7366\n\n     --------\n\nFirst multiply by 6 (ones place):\n\n955 \u00d76:\n\n5\u00d76=30, write 0 carryover 3.\n\n5\u00d76=30 +3=33, write 3 carryover 3.\n\n9\u00d76=54 +3=57. So first partial product is 5730.\n\nNext, multiply by 6 (tens place, so shifted one position):\n\n9