ps that's the mistake. Let's see:\n\n647\u00d79:\n\n7\u00d79=63, write down 3, carryover 6.\n\n4\u00d79=36 +6=42, write down 2, carryover 4.\n\n6\u00d79=54 +4=58. So the result is 5,823. But if someone added wrong in the hundreds place: 6\u00d79=54 plus the carried over 4 is 58, so that's correct. But if they thought 6\u00d79 +4 is 58, so the thousands digit is 5 and hundreds digit 8, so 5,823. So that's correct. So unless they miscalculated the 4\u00d79 part.\n\nWait, maybe the 4 is in the tens place. 647 is 6 hundreds, 4 tens, 7 ones. So when multiplying by 9:\n\nFirst digit (units): 7\u00d79=63,\n\nSecond digit (tens): 4\u00d79=36 plus carryover 6 makes 42,\n\nThird digit (hundreds): 6\u00d79=54 plus carryover 4 makes 58. So the total is 5,823. So if someone made a mistake in the tens place multiplication, like 4\u00d79=36 but added wrong? Like 36 +6=42, so that's correct. So 5823. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they multiplied 647 by 90 as 647\u00d79\u00d710, but miscalculated 647\u00d79 as 5,823 +30? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they added the three terms incorrectly. Let's see:\n\n3,882\n\n+58,230\n\n=62,112,\n\nThen 62,112 +323,500. Let me add digit by digit:\n\n323,500\n\n+ 62,112\n\nStart from the right:\n\n0 +2 =2,\n\n0 +1=1,\n\n5 +1=6,\n\n3 +2=5,\n\n2 +6=8,\n\n3 +0 (since 62,112 has no hundred thousands place)=3.\n\nWait, wait, writing them out:\n\n323,500\n\n+\u00a0 62,112\n\n=?\n\nLet's align them:\n\n323500\n\n+\u00a0 62112\n\n----------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n0 +2 =2\n\n0 +1 =1\n\n5 +1 =6\n\n3 +2 =5\n\n2 +6 =8\n\n3 +0 =3\n\nSo that gives 385,612. Correct. So the addition is right. So the user's answer is off by 30, probably due to a miscalculation in the second term. Since 385,642 -385,612 =30, that suggests that one of the terms was 30 higher. If the second term was 58,260 instead of 58,230, that would add 30, leading to the total of 385,642. So likely, the user made an error in calculating 647\u00d79 as 5,826 instead of 5,823. \