ck again:\n\nWait, 862 ×700. Alternatively, perhaps I miscalculated 862 ×700. Let me do it another way. 862 ×700 is the same as 862 ×7 ×100. 862 ×7 is 6,034, so 6,034 ×100 is indeed 603,400. That\'s correct.\n\nWait, maybe the user made a mistake? Let me check with a calculator approach. Let\'s compute 862 ×704.\n\nAlternatively, 704 can be broken down as 700 + 4, so 862×700 +862×4. That\'s exactly what I did, which gives 603,400 +3,448 = 606,848. So maybe the user\'s answer of 607,848 is incorrect? Let me verify using another method.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I should compute 862 ×704 directly.\n\nLet me do it step by step:\n\nFirst, write down 862 ×704.\n\nMultiplying 862 by 4: 3,448.\n\nMultiplying 862 by 0 (tens place): 0, but shifted one position to the left (so effectively 00), so adding a zero at the end? Wait no, actually in multiplication, when you do the tens place, you shift one to the left. Wait, perhaps I made an error in the place value.\n\nWait, in standard multiplication:\n\n       862\n     ×704\n     -------\n        3448  (862 ×4)\n       0000   (862 ×0, shifted one position to the left)\n     603400   (862 ×7, shifted two positions to the left)\n     -------\n     Sum them up.\n\nWait, the 7 in 704 is in the hundreds place, so when multiplying by the hundreds digit, you shift two places. So the 862×7 is 6,034, then shifted two places becomes 603,400. The 0 in the tens place, so 862×0 is 0, shifted one place becomes 0. The 4 in the ones place gives 3,448, no shift. So adding 3,448 + 0 + 603,400 = 606,848. That\'s correct.\n\nTherefore the user\'s answer of 607,848 is off by 1,000. Hmm. Maybe they thought that 862 ×700 is 603,400, but then added 3,448 to get 606,848. But the user\'s answer is 607,848. Wait, perhaps I made a mistake in the addition?\n\nWait 603,400 plus 3,448:\n\n603,400 + 3,000 is 606,400, then add 448 more: 606,400 + 448 = 606,848. Correct. So the user\'s answer is 607,848, which is 1,000 more. Maybe they added 603,400 + 4,448? That would give 607,848. But why would they have 4,448? Wait, perhaps they miscalculated the first step as 862×4= 3,448? No, that\'s correct. Wait, perhaps the hundreds place was miscalculated as 862×700= 603,400. But 603,400 +3,448= 606,848. Hmm. Alternatively, maybe the user thought that the hundreds digit was 70 instead of 7? 862×70 is 60,340, but shifted two places would be 603,400. Wait, that\'s the same as before.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the problem setup. Wait, the original question is 862 ×704. Let me check with a calculator. Let me do 862 ×704.\n\nFirst, 862 ×700 = 603,400. 862 ×4 = 3,448. Adding them gives 603,400 +3,448 = 606,848. So that\'s correct. The user\'s answer is 607,848, which is 1,000 more. Maybe they thought that 862 ×700 is 603,400 + 1,000? Not sure. Alternatively, maybe they did 862 ×700 as 862 ×7×100, but 862×7 is 6,034, so 6,034×100 is correct. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps they multiplied 862 ×704 as (800 +62) ×(700 +4). Let\'s see:\n\n(800 +62)(700 +4) = 800×700 +800×4 +62×700 +62×4\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n800×700 = 560,000\n\n800×4 = 3,200\n\n62×700 = 43,400\n\n62×4 = 248\n\nAdding all together:\n\n560,000 +3,200 = 563,200\n\n563,200 +43,400 = 606,600\n\n606,600 +248 = 606,848. Same result. So that\'s correct.\n\nHmm, so the user\'s answer is wrong. The correct answer is 606,848. But in the problem statement, the user says "I think the correct answer is: 607848". So perhaps they made a mistake. Therefore, in the template, the answer should be 606,848. \n\nWait, but maybe I missed a step? Let me check again. Wait, the problem says the user wants me to act as a math teacher and solve it in the given template. The example had three steps because the second number had three digits. Let me make sure I followed that.\n\nThe example had for 27 ×153:\n\n1. Multiply 27 by 3 (ones place) →81\n\n2. Multiply 27 by 50 (tens place) →1350\n\n3. Multiply 27 by 100 (hundreds place) →2700\n\nThen sum: 81+1350+2700 = 4131.\n\nSo simila