usands digit) plus 0 (from 90,500\'s thousands digit) =1, plus the carryover 1 from the hundreds place would make it 2. So thousands place is 2.\n\nThe ten thousands place: 4 +9 =13, so write 3 and carryover 1 to the hundred thousands place, which is 1. So the total becomes 132,130.\n\nHmm, so according to this calculation, the total is 132,130. But the user thought the answer was 132,138. That suggests I made a mistake here, or maybe the user did. Let me try multiplying 905 by 146 using another method to check.\n\nAlternative method: standard multiplication.\n\nMultiply 905 by 146.\n\nFirst, write 905\n\n×146\n\n________\n\nStart with the ones digit (6):\n\n905 ×6: 905×6=5,430.\n\nThen the tens digit (4), but since it\'s the tens place, it\'s 40. So 905×40=36,200.\n\nThen the hundreds digit (1), which is 100: 905×100=90,500.\n\nAdding those together: 5,430 +36,200 =41,630; then +90,500 =132,130. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the user made a mistake. Let me compute 905 ×146 using another approach. Let\'s compute 905×100=90,500; 905×40=36,200; 905×6=5,430. Total same as before.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should do the multiplication vertically:\n\n   905\n\n× 146\n\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 905 by 6: 5430.\n\nThen multiply 905 by 40 (which is 4 shifted one position): 905×4=3620, then add a zero →36200.\n\nThen multiply 905 by 100 (shifted two positions): 90500.\n\nAdd them up:\n\n5430\n\n36200\n\n90500\n\nTotal: 5430+36200 =41630; 41630+90500=132,130.\n\nHmm, so that\'s consistent. So perhaps the user\'s answer of 132,138 is incorrect. Let me check with calculator.\n\nLet me compute 905 ×146.\n\nFirst, 900×146 = 900×100 +900×40 +900×6 = 90,000 +36,000 +5,400 = 131,400.\n\nThen add 5×146=730. So total is 131,400 +730 = 132,130. Yep, that\'s correct. So the correct answer is 132,130, not 132,138. Therefore the user\'s thought was wrong. But the problem says "I think the correct answer is: 132138". So perhaps there\'s a miscalculation here.\n\nWait, maybe I misread the numbers. Let me check again the original problem: "What is the product of 905 and 146?" The user\'s answer is 132,138. Let me do 905×146 again step by step.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the breakdown. Let me do it as:\n\n905 ×146 =905×(100+40+6)=905×100 +905×40 +905×6 =90,500 +36,200 +5,430.\n\nAdding those three numbers:\n\n90,500 +36,200 = 126,700. Then 126,700 +5,430 =132,130. Yes, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem was written differently. Wait, perhaps the numbers were 905 and 146? Let me check with a calculator.\n\nCalculating 905 ×146:\n\nLet me compute 905×146:\n\nFirst, 905×100=90,500\n\n905×40=36,200\n\n905×6=5,430\n\nTotal: 90,500 +36,200 =126,700; plus 5,430 gives 132,130. So the correct answer is 132,130.\n\nTherefore, the user\'s thought of 132,138 is incorrect. Maybe they added wrong. Let me see where 132,138 would come from. Suppose instead of 5,430 +36,200 =41,630, then adding 90,500. If someone added 41,630 +90,500 as 132,130. But 132,138 is 8 more. Hmm, perhaps a miscalculation in one of the multiplications.\n\nAlternatively, maybe in multiplying 905×40, if someone did 905×4=3,620, then multiplied by 10 to get 36,200. That\'s correct. Alternatively, if they miscalculated 905×4 as 3,620. 900×4=3,600, plus 5×4=20 gives 3,620. Correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe in the 905×6 step, if someone thought 900×6=5,400 plus 5×6=30 is 5,430. Correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe adding 5,430 +36,200:\n\nIf someone did 5,430 +36,200:\n\n5,430 + 36,200. Let\'s see, 5,000 +36,000=41,000. 430+200=630. So total 41,630. Then adding 90,500. 41,630 +90,500. 41,630+90,000=131,630. Then +500 gives 132,130.\n\nHmm, so unless the user added 5,430 +36,200 incorrectly as 41,630+90,500 but messed up the addition. Let me see 41,630 +90,500:\n\n41,630 + 90,500:\n\nAdding the thousands: 41,000 +90,000 =131,000. Then 630 +500 =1,130. So total 132,130.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user thought that 905×146 is (900×146) + (5×146). Let\'s see:\n\n900×146: 900×100=90,000; 900×40=36,000; 900×6=5,400 → total 90,000+36,000=126,000+5,400=131,400.\n\nThen 5×146: 5×100=500; 5×40=200; 5×6=30 → total 500+200=700+30=730.\n\nTotal is 131,400 +730=132,130. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user thought 146 as 150-4. Let\'s see:\n\n905×146 =905×(150-4)=905×150 -905×4.\n\n905×150: 905×100=90,500; 905×50=45,250 → total 90,500+45,250=135,750.\n\nThen 905×4=3,620. So 135,750 -3,620=132,130. Still the same.\n\nHmm, so I think the user\'s answer of 132,138 is incorrect. The correct answer should be 132,130. Perhaps there was a typo in the user\'s thought. Let me check again if I made a mistake in the problem\'s numbers. The problem states 905 and 146. Yes. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user multiplied 905×146 as follows, making an error in one step:\n\nSuppose they did 905×6=5,430. Then 905×4=3,620 but then instead of multiplying by 10 (making it 36,200), they multiplied by 40 and added something wrong. Alternatively, maybe they thought 905×40 as 905×4×10=3,620×10=36,200. That\'s correct. Alternatively, if they added wrong: 5,430 +36,200 =41,630, then added to 90,500 as 41,630 +90,500. Let\'s see, maybe they added 41,630 + 90,500 as 132,130, but the user thought it was 132,138. Hmm, difference is 8. Maybe they added 5,430 +36,200 as 41,638 instead of 41,630. Let\'s see, 5,430+36,200: if someone messed up the 5,430 as 5,438, then adding would give 41,638. Then adding 90,500 would get 132,138. Maybe that\'s the mistake. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps in multiplying 905×6, if someone thought 9×6=54, so 900×6=5,400; then 5×6=30, so total 5,400+30=5,430. Correct. But if they thought 5,430 plus 36,200 gives 41,630. \n\nAlternatively, maybe when adding 41,630 and 90,500, they did 41,630 +90,500 as follows: 41,630 + 90,000 =131,630, then plus 500 is 132,130. So unless they added 41,630 +90,500 as 132,130. So the user\'s thought of 132,138 must be an error. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 132,130. So the user\'s initial thought was wrong.