+15,158. If they thought 620,100 +15,158 = 635,258. So that\'s correct. So unless they did 620,100 minus 15,000? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the user miscalculated 689 ×20 as 12,780 instead of 13,780. Let me see:\n\nIf the second partial product was 12,780 instead of 13,780, then total would be 1,378 +12,780 =14,158. Then adding to 620,100: 620,100 +14,158 =634,258. Still not 615k.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps they did 689 ×20 as 13,780 (correct), but then 689 ×900 as 610,100 (if they messed up the 689×9 as 6,101), then 610,100 +13,780 =623,880 +1,378 =625,258. Still not matching.\n\nHmm. Alternatively, maybe the user had a different multiplication step. Let me try multiplying 689 ×922 another way.\n\nLet me compute 689 ×922 as (700 -11) × (900 +22). Wait, maybe that\'s overcomplicating, but let\'s see:\n\nAlternatively, 689 ×922 = 689×(900 +20 +2) which is what we did before.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should try to compute 689 ×922 directly in another method.\n\nLet me do 689 ×922:\n\nMultiply 689 by 922:\n\nFirst, multiply 689 ×2 =1,378.\n\nMultiply 689 ×20 =13,780.\n\nMultiply 689 ×900 =620,100.\n\nTotal is 1,378 +13,780 =15,158 +620,100 =635,258. So that\'s consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user thought that 689 ×922 is (600 +80 +9) × (900 +20 +2). Let\'s try expanding that.\n\n(600 +80 +9)(900 +20 +2) = 600×900 +600×20 +600×2 +80×900 +80×20 +80×2 +9×900 +9×20 +9×2.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n600×900 = 540,000,\n\n600×20 =12,000,\n\n600×2 =1,200,\n\n80×900 =72,000,\n\n80×20 =1,600,\n\n80×2 =160,\n\n9×900 =8,100,\n\n9×20 =180,\n\n9×2 =18.\n\nNow add all these together:\n\nStart with the largest terms:\n\n540,000 +72,000 =612,000,\n\n+8,100 =620,100,\n\n+12,000 =632,100,\n\n+1,600 =633,700,\n\n+1,200 =634,900,\n\n+180 =635,080,\n\n+160 =635,240,\n\n+1,200? Wait, no, let me step by step:\n\nWait, let me list all terms:\n\n540,000\n\n+12,000 → 552,000\n\n+1,200 → 553,200\n\n+72,000 → 625,200\n\n+1,600 → 626,800\n\n+160 →626,960\n\n+8,100 →635,060\n\n+180 →6