g 271 is the multiplier; actually, in multiplication, it doesn\'t matter which is first, but let me see):\n\nWait, actually, in standard multiplication, you can arrange either number as the top. Let me set it up as:\n\n      271\n    × 171\n    -------\n\nMultiply 271 by 1 (the ones digit of 171):\n\n271 ×1 =271.\n\nThen multiply 271 by 70 (the tens digit, which is 7 in the tens place, so 7×10). \n\n271 ×7 = 1,897. Then add a zero to get 18,970.\n\nThen multiply 271 by 100 (the hundreds digit of 171, which is 1 in the hundreds place). \n\n271 ×100 =27,100.\n\nNow add them all up: 271 +18,970 +27,100.\n\nAdding 271 +18,970 first: 271 +18,970 = 19,241.\n\nThen add 27,100: 19,241 +27,100 =46,341.\n\nHmm, so that\'s the same result as before. But the user\'s initial answer was 46,347, which is 6 more. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, perhaps I misread the original numbers? Let me check the question again. The user wrote:\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 171 and 271?\nI think the correct answer is: 46341 or 46347?\n\nWait, the user\'s initial answer is 46,347. But according to my calculations, it\'s 46,341. Let me check with a calculator.\n\nWait, 171 × 271. Let me compute this step by step:\n\n171 × 200 = 34,200\n\n171 ×70 = 11,970\n\n171 ×1 =171\n\nAdding them together: 34,200 +11,970 =46,170, then +171 =46,341. So that\'s correct.\n\nAlternatively, 271 ×171:\n\n271 ×100 =27,100\n\n271 ×70 =18,970\n\n271 ×1 =271\n\n27,100 +18,970 =46,070 +271 =46,341. Yep, same.\n\nTherefore, the user\'s initial thought of 46,347 must be wrong. The correct answer is 46,341. So maybe there\'s a typo in the question? Or perhaps the numbers were different? Let me check the original problem again.\n\nWait, the user\'s problem is: What is the product of 171 and 271? So numbers are 171 and 271. The user\'s own answer is 46347, but that\'s incorrect. So I need to point out that the correct answer is 46,341. But according to the initial problem template, the user wants me to follow the 