000 =362,400, plus 816 is 363,216. So that\'s 363,216. Hmm, but the user\'s answer is 363,716. That suggests I might have miscalculated.\n\nWait, maybe I made an error in the hundreds place digit? Wait, 966\'s hundreds digit is 9, so 900. Correct. So that step is okay.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the user\'s suggested answer is wrong, or maybe I messed up in the multiplication steps?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user\'s thought of 363,716 is incorrect, and the correct answer is 363,216?\n\nWait, let me try multiplying 376 ×966 directly using another method to check.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s compute 376 × 966 using the standard algorithm:\n\n\u2003\u2003376\n×\u202f966\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 376 by 6 (units place):\n\n376 ×6 = 2256.\n\nThen multiply 376 by 60 (tens place, so shift one position):\n\n376 ×60 = 22560.\n\nThen multiply 376 by 900 (hundreds place, shift two positions):\n\n376 ×900 = 338400.\n\nNow add them all together:\n\n2256 (units)\n22560 (tens)\n338400 (hundreds)\nTotal: 2256 + 22560 = 24816; 24816 + 338400 = 363,216.\n\nHmm, so that\'s 363,216. But the user\'s answer is 363,716. There\'s a discrepancy here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I miscalculated 376 × 900?\n\n376 ×9: 300×9=2700, 70×9=630, 6×9=54. 2700+630 is 3330, plus 54 is 3384. So 3384 ×100 is 338,400. That\'s correct.\n\nHmm. Alternatively, maybe the original numbers were different? Wait, the question says 376 and 966. The user says they think the correct answer is 363,716. Let me check with a calculator.\n\nWait, let me compute 376 × 966 using another approach.\n\nAlternatively, break down 966 into (900 + 60 +6). So 376×(900+60+6)=376×900 + 376×60 +376×6, which is exactly what I did. So the total is 338,400 +22,560 +2,256 = 363,216.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the user made a mistake in their thought? Let me compute using another method.\n\nLet me compute 376 × 966:\n\nAlternatively, compute 376 × 966 as follows:\n\nFirst, 376 × 1000 = 376,000.\n\nSubtract 376 × 34 (since 1000 - 34