\n2 3 7 9 0 0\n\nand 19,032 is:\n\n0 1 9 0 3 2\n\nWait, aligning:\n\nHundred thousands: 2 vs 0\n\nTen thousands: 3 vs 1\n\nThousands:7 vs9\n\nHundreds:9 vs0\n\nTens:0 vs3\n\nUnits:0 vs2\n\nWait, perhaps better to write:\n\n237900\n\n+\u00a0 19032\n\n=?\n\nAdding step by step:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n0 +2 =2\n\n0 (tens place) +3 =3\n\n9 (hundreds) +0 (hundreds of 19032?) Wait, 19032 has:\n\nThe digits are:\n\n1 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 2 (units). Wait, no, 19,032 is 1 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 2 (units). So when adding to 237,900 (which is 2 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),9 (hundreds),0 (tens),0 (units)), the alignment is:\n\n237900\n\n+\u00a0 19032\n\n------------\n\nLet me write them aligned properly:\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 237900\n\n+\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 19032\n\n------------\n\nAdding each column:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 0 +2 =2\n\nTens: 0 +3 =3\n\nHundreds:9 +0 =9\n\nThousands:7 +9 =16. Write down 6, carry over 1.\n\nTen-thousands:3 +1 (from the 19032's ten thousands digit?) Wait, the 19032's ten thousands digit is 1 (the first digit). Wait, the second number (19032) is only five digits, so when added to 237900 (which is six digits), the alignment is:\n\nHundred thousands: 2 vs 0\n\nTen thousands: 3 vs 1 (from the 1 in 19032)\n\nThousands:7 vs9 (the second digit in 19032 is 9)\n\nHundreds:9 vs0\n\nTens:0 vs3\n\nUnits:0 vs2\n\nWait, maybe better to think:\n\n237900 is:\n\n2 (hundred thousand)\n\n3 (ten thousand)\n\n7 (thousand)\n\n9 (hundred)\n\n0 (ten)\n\n0 (unit)\n\n19032 is:\n\n1 (ten thousand)\n\n9 (thousand)\n\n0 (hundred)\n\n3 (ten)\n\n2 (unit)\n\nSo when adding:\n\nHundred thousand place: 2 +0 =2\n\nTen thousand place:3 +1=4\n\nThousand place:7 +9=16\n\nHundred place:9 +0=9\n\nTen place:0 +3=3\n\nUnit place:0 +2=2\n\nWait, but the thousand place gives 16, so we have to carry over. Let's do it step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 0 +2 =2\n\nTens:0 +3 =3\n\nHundreds:9 +0=9\n\nThousands:7 +9=16. So write 6 and carry over 1 to the ten thousands.\n\nTen thousands:3 +1 (from the carried) +1 (from the 19032's ten thousand digit?) Wait, the ten thousand digit of the second number is 1. Wait, the ten thousand place is the second digit from the left in 19032? Wait, no. Let me clarify the places:\n\nNumber 237900:\n\nDigit positions:\n\nDigit 1 (leftmost): 2 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit 2: 3 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit3:7 (thousands)\n\nDigit4:9 (hundreds)\n\nDigit5:0 (tens)\n\nDigit6:0 (units)\n\nNumber 19032:\n\nDigit1 (leftmost):1 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit2:9 (thousands)\n\nDigit3:0 (hundreds)\n\nDigit4:3 (tens)\n\nDigit5:2 (units)\n\nSo when adding, the alignment would be:\n\nHundred thousands place: 2 (from first number) and 0 (second number)\n\nTen thousands place: 3 (first) +1 (second) =4\n\nThousands place:7 (first) +9 (second) =16 \u2192 6 with carryover 1\n\nHundreds place:9 (first) +0 (second) + carryover 1 =10 \u2192 0 with carryover 1\n\nTens place:0 (first) +3 (second) + carryover 1=4\n\nUnits place:0 +2=2\n\nWait, hold on, this is getting confusing. Let me do it step by step with each digit:\n\nLet me write both numbers with commas for clarity:\n\n237,900\n\n+\u00a0 19,032\n\n------------\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart from the rightmost digit:\n\nUnits: 0 + 2 =2\n\nTens: 0 +3=3\n\nHundreds:9 +0=9\n\nThousands:7 +9=16. Write down 6, carryover 1 to the next (ten thousands place).\n\nTen thousands place:3 (from first number) +1 (from second number) +1 (carryover) =5?\n\nWait, no, the ten thousands digit of the first number is 3, and the ten thousands digit of the second number is 1 (since 19,032 is 1 ten thousand, 9 thousand...). So adding the ten thousands digits: 3 +1 =4, plus the carryover 1 from thousands place gives 5. So ten thousands place is 5, and no further carryover.\n\nHundred thousands place: only 2 from the first number.\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\nHundred thousands: 2\n\nTen