ht 606,600 +66,726 as:\n\n606,600 +60,000 =666,600, plus 6,726 would be 673,326. \n\nHmm, I can't see how they got 673,322 unless there was an error in the hundreds place addition. Let me try adding the numbers digit by digit again:\n\n66,726\n\n+606,600\n\nAlign the numbers properly:\n\n    66,726\n\n  +606,600\n\n  =----------\n\nStart from the right:\n\nUnits place:6+0=6\n\nTens place:2+0=2\n\nHundreds place:7+6=13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place:6 (from first number) +6 (from second number's thousands place) +1 (carryover) =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nTen-thousands place:6 (first number's 6 in 66,726 is in the ten thousands place?) Wait, let me clarify the digit positions.\n\nWait, 66,726 is written as 6 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 6 (units). \n\n606,600 is 6 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 0 (units).\n\nSo when adding:\n\n- Units:6+0=6\n\n- Tens:2+0=2\n\n- Hundreds:7 (from first) +6 (from second) =13 → 3, carryover 1\n\n- Thousands:6 (first) +6 (second) +1 (carryover) =13 →3, carryover 1\n\n- Ten thousands place:6 (first) +0 (second) +1 (carryover) =7\n\n- Hundred thousands place:0 (first) +6 (second) =6\n\nSo the result is 6 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 6 (units). So 673,326.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 673,326. The user's thought of 673,322 must have a mistake in their calculation, perhaps in the thousands place addition where they got 12 instead of 13? Let's see: if in thousands place they added 6+6=12 and forgot the carryover 1 from the hundreds place, then they would have 12, which gives 2 with carryover 1, leading to 6 (ten thousands place) +0 +1=7, but the thousands digit would be 2 instead of 3. So the number would be 673,226? Not quite. Alternatively, maybe they messed up the hundreds place.\n\nWait, if in hundreds place they had 7+6=13, wrote 3, carryover 1. Then thousands place:6+6+1=13, so write 3, carryover