final_output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 349 and 511, we break down the multiplication into steps based on the place values of the digits in 511.\n\n1. **Multiply 349 by the ones place digit (1):**\n   \\[\n   349 \\times 1 = 349\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 349 by the tens place digit (10):**\n   \\[\n   349 \\times 10 = 3490\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 349 by the hundreds place digit (500):**\n   \\[\n   349 \\times 500 = 174500\n   \\]\n\nNext, we sum all the partial products:\n\\[\n349 + 3490 + 174500\n\\]\n\nAdding these step-by-step:\n- First, add \\(349\\) and \\(3490\\):\n  \\[\n  349 + 3490 = 3839\n  \\]\n- Then, add the result to \\(174500\\):\n  \\[\n  3839 + 174500 = 178339\n  \\]\n\nThus, the final computed product is \\(\\boxed{178339}\\).', '177,500 + 839 = 178,339? Wait, wait, let me verify. .\n\nWait, 174,500 plus 3,839. .\n\nBreaking it down: 174,500 + 3,000 = 177,500. Then add 839 more. 177,500 + 800 = 178,300, plus 39 is 178,339. So total is 178,339. .\n\nHmm, but the user wrote that the correct answer is 174,339. Wait, that\'s conflicting. Did I make a mistake somewhere?\n\nWait, let me double-check each step. Let\'s start over, perhaps I miscalculated one of the partial products.\n\nFirst, 349 × 511. Let me write it out vertically:\n\n      349\n    ×511\n    -------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nFirst, multiply by the ones place (1):\n\n349 × 1 = 349\n\nThen, the tens place (1), but shifted one position to the left (since it\'s the tens place):\n\n349 × 1 (tens place) = 349 ×10 = 3,490\n\nThen, the hundreds place (5), shifted two positions (since it\'s hundreds):\n\n349 × 5 (hundreds place) = 349 ×500 = 174,500\n\nAdding all three:\n\n349 + 3,490 = 3,839\n\n3,839 + 174,500 = 178,339\n\nHmm, so according to this calculation, the answer should be 178,339. But the user says they think the correct answer is 174,339. That\'s a difference of 4,000. Where did I go wrong?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the hundreds place multiplication. Let