f 135,300 must be incorrect. Wait, but where could they have gone wrong?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user made a mistake in the multiplication steps. Let me see. Perhaps they thought that 210 is 21×10, so 930×21×10. Let's compute 930×21 first. 930×20 = 18,600; 930×1 = 930. Adding gives 18,600 + 930 = 19,530. Then multiply by 10 gives 195,300. So same result.\n\nAlternatively, if they thought 930 × 210 is 930 × 200 + 930 × 10. Which is 186,000 + 9,300 = 195,300. So that's correct.\n\nHmm, perhaps the user miscalculated. Maybe they thought 930 × 210 as (900 + 30) × (200 + 10). Let me try that.\n\nExpanding (900 + 30)(200 + 10) = 900×200 + 900×10 + 30×200 + 30×10.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n900×200 = 180,000\n\n900×10 = 9,000\n\n30×200 = 6,000\n\n30×10 = 300\n\nAdding these together: 180,000 + 9,000 = 189,000; 189,000 + 6,000 = 195,000; 195,000 + 300 = 195,300. Same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user made a mistake in their calculation steps. Let me see their thought process. They wrote their answer as 135,300. Let me see if that number makes sense. Let's see: 135,300 divided by 210 is 135,300 ÷ 210. Let's compute that.\n\nDivide numerator and denominator by 10: 13,530 ÷ 21. Let's see, 21 × 600 = 12,600. Subtract that from 13,530: 13,530 - 12,600 = 930. 21 × 44 = 924. So 600 + 44 = 644, and 930 - 924 = 6. So it's 644.285... So 210 × 644 = 135,240, which is close but not exactly. Hmm. Alternatively, maybe they miscalculated the partial products.\n\nWait, maybe they multiplied 930 by 15 instead of 210? 930 × 150 = 139,500? Not sure. Alternatively, perhaps they thought 210 is 15 × 14, but that complicates. Alternatively, maybe they did 93 × 21 instead of 930 × 210. Let's see: 93 ×21. 90×20=1800, 90×1=90, 3×20=60, 3×1=3. Adding those: 1800+90=1890, 1890+60=1950, +3=1953. Then, since they removed two zeros (one from 930 and one from 210), so total two zeros, so adding back two zeros gives 195,300. So that would be correct. But if they only added one zero, they get 1