ernatively, maybe they added 5208 + 6510 + 195,300 as 5208+6510=11,718 plus 195,300= 207,018. So unless they thought 651×300 was 195,600? Let\'s see 651×3=1953, so 300×651 would be 195,300. If they thought 651×3=1956, that would be 195,600. Then adding 5208+6510=11,718 +195,600=207,318. Still not 207,618.\n\nWait, maybe they did 651×318 as 651×300=195,300; 651×18= 651×10 +651×8=6,510+5,208=11,718. Then adding those gives 195,300 +11,718=207,018. So that\'s correct. Alternatively, maybe they thought that the tens digit is 100? Wait, no. Alternatively, perhaps they multiplied 651×318 as (600+51)×(300+18), but that would be more complicated.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they added 5208 +6510 +195,300 incorrectly. Let me check 5208 +6510 again. 5208 +6000 is 11,208. Then add 510 to get 11,718. Then adding 195,300: 195,300 +11,718. Let\'s add 195,300 +10,000=205,300, then +1,718: 205,300+1,700=207,000 plus 18 gives 207,018. Yes. So that\'s correct.\n\nHmm, so perhaps the user\'s thought answer is wrong. The correct answer should be 207,018. But maybe I made a mistake. Let me try multiplying 651×318 in another way. Let\'s do 651 × 318.\n\nAlternatively, 318 is 300 +18. So 651×300=195,300. 651×18: 651×10=6,510; 651×8=5,208. So 6,510+5,208=11,718. Then total is 195,300 +11,718=207,018. Yes.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using calculator: 651 × 318. Let me compute 651 × 300 = 195,300; 651 × 18 = let\'s see 651×10=6,510, 651×8=5,208; adding those gives 11,718. Total 195,300 +11,718=207,018. So that\'s correct.\n\nTherefore, the user\'s thought answer of 207,618 is incorrect. The correct answer should be 207,018. So the problem might have a mistake in the user\'s expected answer. But according to the problem statement, the user wants me to solve it as per the template. The user\'s question says they think the correct answer is 207,618 but according to calculations it\'s 207,018. Maybe they made an error in their own calculation, but I need to follow the template.\n\nWait, looking back at